{"signature":{"source":"NASA/JPL Horizons API","version":"1.2"},"result":"*******************************************************************************\n Revised: Sep 01, 2026           SOHO Spacecraft  / (Earth)                 -21\n                       http://sohowww.nascom.nasa.gov/about/about.html\n\n BACKGROUND:\n  SOHO, the Solar & Heliospheric Observatory, is a collaboration between ESA \n   and NASA to study the Sun, from deep core to the outer corona, and solar \n   particle wind.\n\n  SOHO was launched by an Atlas II-AS (AC-121) from Cape Canaveral Air Station \n   on December 2, 1995, at 08:08 UT.\n\n  SOHO orbits the Sun with the Earth, slowly looping around the First \n   Lagrangian Point (L1). At L1, the combined gravity of the Earth and Sun \n   balance, keeping the spacecraft on an Earth-Sun line. L1 is approximately \n   1.5 million km from Earth in the direction of the Sun (about four times \n   the distance of the Moon). SOHO therefore has a continuous view of the Sun. \n\n  An unexpected loss of contact with SOHO occurred on 25 June 1998. The mission\n   was recovered and normal operations resumed in mid-November 1998. Despite \n   subsequent failures of all three gyroscopes (the last in December 1998), \n   new gyro-less control software installed by February 1999, allowing SOHO to \n   resume normal operations (and making SOHO the first 3-axis-stabilised \n   spacecraft to operate without a gyroscope).\n\n MISSION ACCOMPLISHMENTS:\n  * Provided the first images of structures and flows below the Sun's surface \n     and of activity on the far side\n  * Revealed the Sun's extremely dynamic atmosphere\n  * Provided evidence for the transfer of magnetic energy from the surface to\n     the outer solar atmosphere, the corona, through a \"magnetic carpet\"\n  * Identified the source regions of the fast solar wind\n  * New understanding of solar-terrestrial interactions and dramatic\n     improvement of space weather-forecasting based on its continuous stream \n     of images covering the atmosphere, extended corona, and far side\n  * Findings are in over 2500 papers in refereed journals since launch, \n     representing the work of over 2300 individual scientists. \n  * Discovery of over 1100 Sun-grazing comets\n  * Easily accessible data sets\n\n SPACECRAFT PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTICS:\n  SOHO has two modules: \n   - Service Module: forms the lower portion of the spacecraft.\n     Provides power, thermal control, pointing, telecommunications,\n      and support for the solar panels. \n   - Payload Module: above Service Module, housing all scientific \n      instruments.\n   * Dimensions (height, breadth, width): 4.3 x 2.7 x 3.7 m\n   * Width with solar array deployed: 9.5 m\n   * Total mass at launch: 1850 kg\n   * Payload: 610 kg\n   * Telemetry during real-time operation: 200 Kbits/s\n   * Telemetry during on-board storage mode: 40 Kbits/s\n  Science instruments (12)\n   * CDS (Coronal Diagnostic Spectrometer)\n   * CELIAS (Charge, Element, and Isotope Analysis System)\n   * COSTEP (Comprehensive Suprathermal and Energetic Particle Analyzer)\n   * EIT (Extreme ultraviolet Imaging Telescope)\n   * ERNE (Energetic and Relativistic Nuclei and Electron experiment)\n   * GOLF (Global Oscillations at Low Frequencies)\n   * LASCO (Large Angle and Spectrometric Coronagraph)\n   * MDI (Michelson Doppler Imager)\n   * SUMER (Solar Ultraviolet Measurements of Emitted Radiation)\n   * SWAN (Solar Wind Anisotropies)\n   * UVCS (Ultraviolet Coronagraph Spectrometer)\n   * VIRGO (Variability of Solar Irradiance and Gravity Oscillations)\n\n SPACECRAFT TRAJECTORY (concatenated trajectories from GSFC FDF):\n\n  For the time-span 1998-Aug-19 to 1998-Sep-25, there is no official \n  trajectory information due to the recovery effort. Therefore, the initial \n  state at resumption of operations 1998-Sep-25 was integrated back to \n  August 19 assuming a purely ballistic trajectory. Trajectory errors \n  during this interval may be significant due to unmodeled dynamics.\n\n  Trajectory name             Start        Stop\n  --------------------------  -----------  -----------\n  soho_1995                   1995-Dec-02  1996-Jan-01\n  soho_1996                   1996-Jan-01  1997-Jan-01\n  soho_1997                   1997-Jan-01  1998-Jan-01\n  soho_1998a                  1998-Jan-01  1998-Aug-19\n  soho_1998a-b_gap            1998-Aug-19  1998-Sep-25 (ballistic filler)\n  soho_1998b                  1998-Sep-25  1999-Jan-01 (recovery of contact)\n  soho_1999                   1999-Jan-01  2000-Jan-01\n  soho_20**                   2000-Jan-01  2017-Jan-01\n  soho_2017                   2017-Jan-01  2018-Jan-01 (prediction Dec20-Jan01)\n  soho_2018                   2018-Jan-01  2019-Jan-01 (prediction Dec17-Jan01)\n  soho_2019                   2019-Jan-01  2020-Jan-01\n  soho_2020                   2020-Jan-01  2021-Jan-01 \n  soho_2021                   2021-Jan-01  2022-Jan-01 (prediction Dec14-Jan01)\n  soho_2022                   2022-Jan-01  2023-Jan-01\n  soho_2023                   2023-Jan-01  2023-Jan-01 (prediction > Dec 13)\n  soho_2024                   2024-Jan-01  2025-Jan-01 (prediction > Dec 16)\n  soho_2025                   2025-Jan-01  2026-Jan-01 (prediction > Nov 04)\n  soho_2026                   2026-Jan-01  2026-Nov-02 (prediction > Aug 26)\n*******************************************************************************\n\n\n*******************************************************************************\nEphemeris / API_USER Tue Oct 27 09:16:55 2026 Pasadena, USA      / Horizons\n*******************************************************************************\nTarget body name: SOHO (spacecraft) (-21)         {source: SOHO_merged}\nCenter body name: Earth (399)                     {source: DE441}\nCenter-site name: BODY CENTER\n*******************************************************************************\nStart time      : A.D. 1995-Dec-02 08:08:01.0000 UT \nStop  